course: dictionary
lesson: 15709
title: Tot: one word, three meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word can have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one to use. Tot is a good example. Read the words around it, because they are its context and they leave clues.
explain: «The tiny tot giggled at the bubbles.» Tiny and giggled are the clues. A tot is a very small child, a toddler. Put that meaning back into the sentence to check it fits.
explain: «Let's tot up the shopping before we pay.» Here tot up means add up the numbers to find the total. The word up after tot is a clue that the meaning is about adding.
fact: Tot can be a noun (a tot, tots) or a verb (tot up, tots up, totted up, totting up).
fact: «Tot» means a very small child, a toddler, as in «a play area for tots».
fact: «Tot up» means add up numbers to find a total.
fact: In old sea stories, a tot is a small amount of strong drink, such as the daily tot of rum given to sailors.
fact: Tot up is mostly used in the UK. When you meet a new meaning, check the words around it and, if you are unsure, use a dictionary.
exercise: «The little tot climbed onto the slide.» What is a tot here? | A type of slide | A small glass of drink | A grown-up in a hurry | *A very small child
exercise: «Can you tot up the cost of these apples?» What does tot up mean? | *Add up to find the total | Throw away | Wrap up warm | Eat quickly
exercise: «The tots built a sandcastle with their spades.» Which clue tells you the tots are young? | *Their spades and sandcastle, which children play with | The tots are sailors | The tots are very tall | The tots are sold in shops
test: «Sailors in the old days were given a tot of rum each day.» What is a tot here? | A tiny boat | A kind of rope | A tired sailor | *A small measure of strong drink
test: Mum says, «Tot up the points on your score card.» What does she want you to do? | Count how many players there are | *Add the points to find the total | Throw the card away | Hide the score card
exam: «The tot was too small to reach the tap, so Dad lifted him up.» What is a tot in this sentence? | A grown-up | A small boat | A type of tap | *A very small child
